# 8. SUBJECTS — SYLLABUS AND TOPIC TRACKER

The Subjects page is a core differentiator of MODULUS.

Build a structured academic syllabus manager that allows students to understand and track their syllabus at chapter and topic level.

## A. Subject management

Each subject should contain:
- Subject name
- Course code, optionally
- Semester
- Optional instructor
- Subject colour
- Optional credits
- Chapters and topics
- Linked classes
- Linked assignments
- Linked examinations
- Linked study tasks
- Progress summary

Allow users to:
- Create subjects
- Edit subjects
- Archive subjects
- Restore archived subjects
- Reorder subjects
- Delete subjects with confirmation
- Search and filter subjects

## B. Syllabus hierarchy

Support the following hierarchy:

Subject → Unit → Chapter → Topic → Optional subtopic.

The hierarchy should remain flexible because academic syllabuses vary between institutions.

Allow students to use units or chapters without requiring every level.

## C. Topic tracking

Each topic should have one of these states:
- Not Started
- In Progress
- Completed
- Needs Revision

Allow students to change states manually.

Each topic may also include:
- Estimated study duration
- Optional personal notes
- Last-studied date
- Revision count
- Optional next revision date
- Optional difficulty rating
- Position within the chapter

Do not store uploaded lecture notes or textbooks against topics. Allow links or brief personal notes where appropriate, but do not turn the product into a file repository.

## D. Syllabus checklist

Provide:
- Individual topic checkboxes
- Chapter completion summaries
- Expandable and collapsible chapters
- Expand/collapse all
- Topic search
- Bulk topic creation
- Drag-and-drop reordering where appropriate
- Quick status changes
- Undo accidental changes
- Clear visual states
- Keyboard-accessible controls

## E. Syllabus PDF import

Allow a student to upload a syllabus PDF.

Extract:
- Subject names
- Units
- Chapters
- Topics
- Subtopics, when identifiable

Use the following workflow:

1. Upload and validate the PDF.
2. Extract text and relevant structure.
3. Use OCR if necessary and supported.
4. Use AI only when required to interpret the syllabus structure.
5. Produce structured data matching a defined schema.
6. Show the extracted hierarchy.
7. Allow the student to edit, add, remove, and reorder entries.
8. Save the syllabus only after confirmation.

Preserve source references during extraction where practical.

Never invent a chapter or topic simply to fill a missing section.

## F. Progress calculation

Use deterministic calculations.

Chapter completion:

Completed topics divided by total applicable topics.

Subject completion:

Completed topics across the subject divided by total applicable topics.

Overall syllabus completion:

Completed topics across active subjects divided by total applicable topics across those subjects.

Define how empty chapters and subjects are handled so they do not produce division-by-zero errors or misleading percentages.

Treat archived subjects according to a documented, consistent rule.

Display progress as:
- Percentage
- Progress bar
- Completed/total count

For example: 12 of 20 topics completed — 60%.

Do not automatically mark parent chapters as completed unless their completion status is derived transparently from their child topics.

## G. Revision tracking

Allow students to mark a topic as needing revision.

Optional revision functionality:
- Set a next revision date
- Track revision count
- Record the last review
- Show topics due for revision
- Display revision reminders
- Remove a topic from the revision list when the student chooses

Use simple configurable revision intervals.

Do not claim that a student has mastered a topic simply because they marked it completed.

## H. Subject micro-features

Implement:
- Subject colour coding
- Chapter-level progress
- Topic-level status
- Recently studied topics
- Last-studied indicator
- Revision counter
- Bulk topic creation from pasted text
- Search across chapters and topics
- Undo completion
