# 17. PDF PROCESSING AND AI ARCHITECTURE

AI has a deliberately restricted role.

## Permitted AI operations

Use AI only to:
1. Read supported timetable PDFs.
2. Read supported syllabus PDFs.
3. Read supported hall ticket PDFs.
4. Extract structured academic information from those documents.
5. Compare newly extracted information with existing academic records.
6. Propose changes to those records.
7. Explain ambiguities in extracted information.

Do not build a general-purpose chatbot, AI tutor, assignment-answer generator, or AI-generated motivational assistant.

## Extraction architecture

Use ordinary PDF text extraction and table parsing first.

Use OCR when the PDF contains scanned pages and OCR is supported.

Use an AI model only where semantic interpretation is necessary.

The extraction pipeline should return schema-validated structured data, not unstructured prose.

Validate:
- Dates
- Times
- Subject names
- Course codes
- Weekdays
- Chapter hierarchy
- Required fields
- Duplicate records

Preserve source references where practical.

## Update approval workflow

When a new PDF appears to contain updated information:

1. Read the new document.
2. Compare it with existing records.
3. Produce a proposed change set.
4. Identify additions, modifications, and potential removals.
5. Show the differences to the student.
6. Identify ambiguous changes.
7. Allow individual approval or rejection.
8. Apply approved changes transactionally.
9. Preserve relevant change history.
10. Provide undo or restoration where practical.

Never silently replace confirmed academic information.

A newly uploaded hall ticket must not automatically delete an examination simply because that examination is absent from the new document.

## AI failure handling

If extraction fails:
- Preserve existing records.
- Explain the failure.
- Allow manual entry.
